  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1111 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nA frontal boundary is bisecting the FA late this evening. Moist\nlow level upslope northeasterly flow is occurring on the north\nside of this front. This weather pattern is conducive for areas\nof dense fog later in the morning. Have issued a Dense Fog\nAdvisory for areas where there is the highest confidence of\nFG+. This foggy area will likely delay warming, resulting in\ncooler highs than compared to non foggy areas. Have gone with\nupper 50s to lower 60s across the western zones, with upper 70s\nto lower 80s south of the front and east of the fog. Otherwise,\nwinds will remain fairly light today. A light NE wind is\nexpected north of the front and light SE to S wind is expected\nsouth of it across the eastern and southern counties.\n\nAttention then turns to Friday. Remnant tropical moisture will\nadvect northward across the southern High Plains and closer to\nKansas. Afternoon PWAT values are expected to be greater than\n1.00\" by Friday afternoon. In addition, an upper level shortwave\ntrof will move across the greater region during the day. This\nwill set the stage for scattered rain showers Friday afternoon\nand evening. Rainfall amounts will be highly variable. The\nhighest PWAT values and best low level moisture will likely\nremain across the southern and southeastern zones. So amounts\nwill vary from 0.05 to 0.25\" across the northern zones to around\n0.50\" or even higher near the Oklahoma border. Severe weather is\nnot expected due to poor lapse rates and meager instability.\n\nOver the weekend, long range models and their ensemble members\nindicate shortwave ridging across the high plains. This would\nresult in a drier forecast just in time for the weekend.\nTemperatures look to be mild with highs in the 60s and lows in\nthe 40s to 50s. Models then indicate another trof developing\nacross the western United States Monday and then drifting\ncloser to our region by Monday evening into Tuesday. Ensembles\nare not in agreement on the evolution of this second system.\nWill it be closed off and deep or will it be a progressive wave?\nRegardless, the NBM continues to have pops for the beginning of\nthe workweek with unsettled weather in the forecast. If the wave\nis more deep and closed off, then the potential for higher wind\nmagnitudes will exist with this system.",
